## Comparison method and actual local checks

The reference mathematical conclusions are the already frozen independent exercise sections, not an author's correct set or the author's own agreement with its keys.

All numeric expectations were compared to the independent rational/decimal results with finite-value checks. All exact expectations were compared to the independent complete relations, sets, interval inclusions and variable conditions. All expression expectations were checked against the four independently derived power-law expressions. No product/native validator, ComputeEngine or MathLive process was invoked.

Comparison-only normalization handled cosmetic LaTeX wrappers, supported bracket spellings, decimal versus simple rational presentations and the recognized equivalent power expressions. It was not used as a replacement for the product's exact-answer normalization/editor contract. In particular, fractional scopes and variable/domain meanings were checked against the independent solution before display-only presentation matching.

The first conservative key-matching pass flagged full-relation answers because it had initially compared some full equations to bare relation signs, and flagged a LaTeX command followed by a digit because a word-boundary pattern was too narrow. A targeted comparison against the actual complete source formulas resolved all these flags. They were comparison-method limitations, not rejected author content and not fabricated successful runtime receipts.

For display matching, 119 judging facts were directly located after presentation normalization; the remaining 16 were checked through the actual grouped-label or simplified-expression presentations:
- 683 groups the six constant-expression comparisons, and its remaining variable cases retain their three alternatives including equality.
- 694 simplifies reciprocal powers before comparing them; these are the same values, not changed problems.
- 696 groups the two below-one and two above-one conclusions rather than repeating each full expression.
- 722 uses the equivalent fractional-exponent presentation.
Together these cover all 135 displayed judging facts. A missing literal substring was not treated as a mathematical defect.

Additional targeted checks compared the requested ungraded tasks, actual curve-order chains (including equivalent reversed greater-than presentations), concrete counterexamples, half-open boundary conditions, and the proof's disclosed convention. The repeated-addend counterexample in 718 was evaluated as a repeated value, not misparsed as two different numbers.

